250	                <div class="pipeline-detail" id="pipeline-step-1"><p><strong>Frame capture.</strong> Surgical video runs at 25 frames a second or more, so a live system has at most 40 ms per frame. Each frame reaches the model as a grid of pixel values.</p><div class="pipeline-models"><span>Example tools</span><ul><li>FFmpeg</li><li>OpenCV</li></ul></div></div>
251	                <div class="pipeline-detail" id="pipeline-step-2"><p><strong>Feature extraction.</strong> A backbone network turns each patch of the frame into a feature vector, a list of numbers that describes what the patch shows.</p><div class="pipeline-models"><span>Example models</span><ul><li>ResNet</li><li>Vision Transformer</li><li>DINOv2</li></ul></div></div>
252	                <div class="pipeline-detail is-active" id="pipeline-step-3"><p><strong>Segmentation.</strong> A segmentation model assigns every pixel a class, such as instrument, tissue, or background, which outlines each region.</p><div class="pipeline-models"><span>Example models</span><ul><li>U-Net</li><li>Mask R-CNN</li><li>SAM</li></ul></div></div>
